coverage:
I read the full LaTeX source (introduction, affine, trapping, permanence sections) and checked every step.

- Affine Lemma 2.1, reproduced from the companion paper:
  - choice of the beta_k and type-k labels;
  - raised-coordinate bound (2.2) and the induction applied to the cube [beta_k, b]^U;
  - off-U competitor argument, using h^{beta_k} = o(h^{beta_{k+1}});
  - the k = m-1 case;
  - closedness of A_lambda, compactness of B_t and the radius rho_t;
  - local decay, the cover and cut ordering, and both shift comparisons (above and below);
  - Corollary 2.2.
- Tolerance E and gap H, and the sign/size implication (3.4).
- Prop 3.1:
  - every label active at c has zero slope;
  - K_h is the intersection of half-spaces;
  - E(0) = 1/2 gives K_h ⊂ int B_{√h};
  - the convexity argument for K_h ⊂ B_h.
- Prop 3.2:
  - labels with slope in S^⊥ cannot be active on P \ K_h;
  - monomial ordering by levels;
  - one upward edge dominates all downward flux, via h^H Σk < min k;
  - the cut-flux decomposition;
  - the uniform δ_h from compactness of the active sets.
- Lemma 3.3: the right-derivative formula, the comparison lemma, forward invariance and global existence, and finite entry.
- Section 4:
  - x^0 ∈ K_{h0};
  - the capped square-root scale iteration h_{j+1} = min(√h_j, h_P), which uses K_{h_j} ⊂ B_{h_{j+1}} and reaches h_P in finitely many steps;
  - K_P = K_{h_P} ∩ (c+S).

Sanity check: at a complex-balanced equilibrium, Prop 3.2 would be contradicted if some active slope outside S^⊥ were present. I worked through 0⇌A: the rate condition h^H Σk < min k rules this out, so the argument is self-consistent.
